<Site
  title="Probe Cloud (preview) - FlutterProbe"
  description="Probe Cloud is a hosted history of your FlutterProbe test runs, in preview. What exists today, what the dashboard shows, and how to become a design partner."
  path="/probe-cloud/"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Preview</p>
    <h1>Probe Cloud</h1>
    <p>Probe Cloud is a hosted place to keep the history of your FlutterProbe test runs and share it with your team. It is a preview, not generally available, and plan details are not final. The CLI, agent and local tools remain free.</p>
    <p><strong>The hosted service is not live yet.</strong> The upload client in the CLI is ready, but there is no live endpoint to receive uploads, so <code>--upload-report</code> cannot reach a service today. Design partners get access when it goes live.</p>

    <h2>What the dashboard shows</h2>
    <p>The sample dashboard shows, for one app:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A pass rate for each day, as a trend.</li>
      <li>Failing tests, newest failure first.</li>
      <li>Unstable tests: tests that passed and also failed or needed a retry.</li>
      <li>The newest commit of each pull request, with a result that compares it with the base branch.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href="/demo/">See the sample dashboard</a>. It shows made-up data from a fixture app: the hosted service is not live, and filters and sign-in are not in the sample.</p>

    <h2>What exists today</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>probe test --upload-report</code> is the client side: it sends a run report to a Probe Cloud endpoint, with CI details (provider, branch, commit) and an optional <code>--upload-label</code>. It needs a live endpoint to work end to end, and it is built for GitHub Actions: other CI systems are not supported for uploads yet. Their JUnit and JSON reports work as before.</li>
      <li><code>probe test --upload-local</code> sends one run from a local simulator, emulator or device, with git facts in place of the CI details. The service keeps it as unverified evidence that never counts toward a pull request's verdict, and only where the team has enabled local uploads.</li>
      <li><code>--upload-dry-run</code> works today and needs no service: it prints exactly what would be sent, so you can inspect it before anything leaves your machine.</li>
      <li>The payload is scrubbed before sending: paths are made relative, secret-looking values are redacted, and screenshots and device identifiers are left out. Uploads are capped at 1 MiB and 5,000 results.</li>
      <li>JUnit and JSON reports for any CI system, with no account needed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Plans</h2>
    <p>Free, Team and Scale, and a pay-what-you-use account called On-The-Go. Team, Scale and On-The-Go are planned and not available yet; see <a href="/pricing/">pricing</a>.</p>

    <h2>Design partners</h2>
    <p>We are looking for the first 10 Flutter teams. Probe Cloud is free for design partners while we test it. When paid plans open, you get 50% off the plan you subscribe to, for as long as you stay subscribed. In return, use it on a real project and send us short notes. This is a beta: we may change or end it.</p>
    <div class="cta" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <a class="btn-p" href="https://github.com/AlphaWaveSystems/flutter-probe/issues/new?template=design-partner.yml">Apply as a design partner</a>
      <a class="btn-s" href="mailto:support@alphawavesystems.com">Apply by email</a>
    </div>
    <p>The GitHub form is a public issue. See <a href="/pricing/#design-partners">pricing</a>, the <a href="/terms/">terms</a> and the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.</p>
  </div>
